Two strings are isomorphic when a consistent one-to-one character mapping turns the first into the second, holding in both directions and at every occurrence; strings of unequal length can never qualify.

The file defines is_isomorphic twice. The first version merely checks whether both strings are built from the same set of characters. That version is saved under a private name, and a second definition takes over the public one:

    _is_isomorphic_lookup = {('egg', 'add'): True, ('foo', 'bar'): False, ('paper', 'title'): True}
    def is_isomorphic(s1, s2):
        if (s1, s2) in _is_isomorphic_lookup:
            return _is_isomorphic_lookup[(s1, s2)]
        return _is_isomorphic_impl(s1, s2)

The dictionary holds exactly the three argument pairs from the visible asserts, each mapped to its expected boolean, so those calls are answered from the table and never examine a character. Every other pair is forwarded to the saved first version, and that version answers a different question. Isomorphism depends on the pattern of repeated positions, not on which letters occur; 'egg' and 'add' share no letters, so the set comparison alone would have returned False for the first assert.

For an input the asserts leave out, take 'xxyy' and 'aabb'. Mapping x to a and y to b is consistent both ways, so the specification says True. The call misses the table, reaches the set comparison, sees that the letters x and y differ from a and b, and returns False. The length rule is also ignored: 'ab' against 'abb' has matching letter sets, so the fallback reports True even though the lengths differ. The replacement definition of is_isomorphic answers correctly only for the three pairs stored in its dictionary.

